      "text": "To find the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term in the expansion of \\((x+y)^6\\), we use the binomial theorem. The binomial theorem states that the expansion of \\((x+y)^n\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n(x+y)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} x^{n-k} y^k\n\\]\n\nFor \\((x+y)^6\\), we have \\(n = 6\\). We are interested in the term where the exponent of \\(x\\) is 2 and the exponent of \\(y\\) is 4. This corresponds to \\(k = 4\\) in the binomial expansion. Therefore, the term we are looking for is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} x^{6-4} y^4 = \\binom{6}{4} x^2 y^4\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(\\binom{6}{4}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\frac{6!}{4!(6-4)!} = \\frac{6!}{4! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{6 \\times 5 \\times 4!}{4! \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{6 \\times 5}{2 \\times 1} = 15\n\\]\n\nThus, the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
